{"id": "3749f8c9-0281-4fb7-843d-9e1591c8903e", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "This matches a built-in disk-diagnosis workflow — I'll run through EC2 status, CloudWatch EBS/instance metrics, CloudTrail changes, AWS FIS, and AWS Health for `app-server-01` to see if EBS is the cause.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_aw4RnCyqF3PZdatLZ1ShXj", "tool_name": "gather_context", "input": {"deep_research": true, "prompt": "{
  "task": "Determine whether Amazon EBS is the cause of a reported disk-write slowdown on EC2 instance named 'app-server-01', and why.",
  "context": "User reports disk writes on instance 'app-server-01' became very slow in the last ~15 minutes. Current time is 2026-10-27T23:28:45Z. This is a name, not an instance ID — resolve it via ec2:DescribeInstances (Name tag or similar) in the agent space default region us-east-1 first; if not found there, check other regions/accounts the agent has access to. T0 (problem start) is unknown precisely — assume approximately 2026-10-27T23:13:45Z but confirm/refine T0 from the metrics themselves. Use a collection window of T0-60min to now (fall back to last 3 hours if T0 can't be pinned down).",
  "methodology": "Follow this read-only diagnostic procedure in order, using only these AWS calls: ec2:DescribeInstances, ec2:DescribeVolumes, ec2:DescribeInstanceTypes, ec2:DescribeVolumesModifications, ec2:DescribeFastSnapshotRestores, ec2:DescribeVolumeStatus, ec2:DescribeInstanceStatus, cloudwatch:GetMetricData, cloudwatch:ListMetrics, cloudtrail:LookupEvents, fis:ListExperiments, fis:ListExperimentResolvedTargets, fis:GetExperimentTemplate, health:DescribeEvents. Never call mutating EC2/EBS actions.\n\n1. INVENTORY: DescribeInstances for the instance (InstanceType, State, EbsOptimized, LaunchTime, BlockDeviceMappings -> VolumeIds/DeviceNames). DescribeVolumes for each attached volume (VolumeType, Size, Iops, Throughput, SnapshotId, CreateTime, MultiAttachEnabled, State, Attachments). DescribeInstanceTypes for the instance type to get Hypervisor (nitro vs xen — use this field, NOT DescribeInstances' Hypervisor field) and EbsInfo.EbsOptimizedInfo (BaselineThroughputInMBps, MaximumThroughputInMBps, BaselineIops, MaximumIops). DescribeVolumesModifications for each volume. DescribeFastSnapshotRestores for any volume with a SnapshotId.\n\n2. STATUS: DescribeVolumeStatus for each volume (VolumeStatus.Status, Details io-enabled/io-performance/initialization-state, Events, InitializationStatusDetails). DescribeInstanceStatus with IncludeAllInstances=true (SystemStatus, InstanceStatus, AttachedEbsStatus.Details[].ImpairedSince).\n\n3. METRICS via cloudwatch:GetMetricData, Period=60 over the window:\n   Per volume (VolumeId dimension only, AWS/EBS): VolumeReadOps/VolumeWriteOps (Sum), VolumeReadBytes/VolumeWriteBytes (Sum), VolumeTotalReadTime/VolumeTotalWriteTime (Sum), VolumeQueueLength (Average), VolumeIdleTime (Sum), BurstBalance (Minimum, gp2/st1/sc1 only), VolumeThroughputPercentage (Average, io1/io2 only).\n   Per volume with BOTH VolumeId and InstanceId dimensions (AWS/EBS, Nitro only): VolumeStalledIOCheck (Maximum), VolumeIOPSExceededCheck (Maximum), VolumeThroughputExceededCheck (Maximum), VolumeAvgReadLatency/VolumeAvgWriteLatency (Average, ms), VolumeAvgIOPS/VolumeAvgThroughput (Average). A query with only VolumeId returns no data for these — must include InstanceId too.\n   Per instance (InstanceId dimension, AWS/EC2): EBSReadOps/EBSWriteOps (Sum), EBSReadBytes/EBSWriteBytes (Sum), EBSIOBalance%/EBSByteBalance% (Minimum, Period=300), InstanceEBSIOPSExceededCheck/InstanceEBSThroughputExceededCheck (Maximum, Nitro only), StatusCheckFailed_AttachedEBS (Maximum, Nitro only).\n   If any GetMetricData call returns empty, call ListMetrics with the namespace/dimension to verify the metric exists before concluding it's not published (record as data gap if genuinely absent — never treat empty as healthy/zero).\n\n4. CHANGES: cloudtrail:LookupEvents for ResourceName=<each volume id>, then ResourceName=<instance id>, then EventName=PauseVolumeIO and EventName=InjectVolumeIOLatency, all over T0-2h to now. Keep every event where readOnly=false (keep eventTime, eventName, eventSource, userIdentity/caller, target resources, request parameters) — not just a fixed list of event names. Also check fis:ListExperiments (keep running experiments or ones created in-window), fis:ListExperimentResolvedTargets for each to see if they target this instance/volume, and fis:GetExperimentTemplate for matched ones to get the action and duration.\n\n5. AWS HEALTH: call health:DescribeEvents in us-east-1 with filter for services EBS and EC2, region = the instance's region, startTimes from T0-2h. If it returns SubscriptionRequiredException, record that as a data gap (Health API needs Business Support+/Enterprise Support/Unified Operations).\n\n6. ANALYSIS — determine which of these findings is confirmed by the data (evaluate all, report primary cause using this priority order top to bottom when multiple are confirmed):\n   F1 Change made in the account: a mutating CloudTrail event on the instance/volume at or up to 2 min before T0 that can explain the symptom (DetachVolume/StopInstances/RebootInstances -> I/O errors; ModifyInstanceAttribute changing instance type -> lower EBS limit; PauseVolumeIO FIS action -> stall; InjectVolumeIOLatency FIS action -> higher latency with ops still completing). OR an FIS experiment running at T0 targeting this resource whose action explains the symptom (even if CloudTrail hasn't delivered the event yet).\n   F2 Stalled volume: VolumeStalledIOCheck=1, or completed ops=0 while VolumeQueueLength>0 for 2+ consecutive minutes, or io-performance=stalled, or AttachedEbsStatus impaired/StatusCheckFailed_AttachedEBS=1 with a signal pointing to the volume.\n   F3 I/O disabled: io-enabled=failed or an event saying I/O disabled due to potential data inconsistency.\n   F4 Instance EBS limit: InstanceEBSThroughputExceededCheck or InstanceEBSIOPSExceededCheck=1 (or instance total flat at instance type's max/baseline where these aren't published) AND no single volume flat at its own limit.\n   F5 Volume burst credits exhausted (gp2/st1/sc1): BurstBalance hit 0 at T0 and IOPS/throughput dropped to baseline after.\n   F6 Volume at its own limit (gp3/io1/io2/gp2/st1/sc1 specific rules — see limit tables) for 5+ minutes, instance not at its own limit.\n   F7 Volume modification in progress that started before T0, state modifying/optimizing, and lowered IOPS/throughput or type below workload demand.\n   F8 Snapshot initialization: volume has SnapshotId, fast snapshot restore not enabled in that AZ, and initialization-state=initializing or Progress<100 (or CreateTime within 24h of T0 for a past problem with mostly-read slowness).\n   F9 Degraded performance: latency after T0 >= 3x median before T0 for 5+ min, ops still completing, F1-F8 all ruled out.\n   F10 EBS is not the cause: F1-F9 all ruled out, every volume status ok, attached EBS check passed, no *ExceededCheck=1 (ignore VolumeIOPSExceededCheck on gp2 while BurstBalance>0), latency/IOPS/throughput after T0 close to medians before T0.\n   If none confirmed, say 'not confirmed' rather than forcing a finding.\n   Compute before/after medians (15 min before T0 vs after T0) for IOPS, throughput (MiB/s for volumes, MB/s for instance), latency (ms), and average I/O size (KiB) to support the finding.\n\n7. Record every data gap encountered (AccessDenied, throttling after retries, genuinely empty/unpublished metrics, volume/instance not found) with the specific call and reason — never silently treat a gap as a healthy signal.",
  "output_requested": "Return: (a) resolved instance ID, region, and volume ID(s)/types/sizes; (b) for each finding F1-F10, whether it is confirmed/ruled out and the supporting evidence with exact metric names, values, and UTC timestamps; (c) the primary cause per the priority order (or 'not confirmed'); (d) latency, IOPS, and throughput before vs after T0 with actual numbers; (e) every mutating CloudTrail event found in the window (time, event, caller, target) — write 'none' if none; (f) any FIS experiments targeting this instance/volume; (g) AWS Health check result or why it couldn't be checked; (h) all data gaps encountered. Do not omit raw numeric evidence — I need the actual values to write the final report myself."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Diagnose EBS/disk I/O slowdown on EC2 instance app-server-01 using EC2, CloudWatch, CloudTrail, FIS, Health."}], "created_at": null}

